A||||11||8Ball||Going shopping in the j/y for a Fiero, any pointers?||||5||
Z||000000||8Ball||08-07-2001||12:09 AM||||Well I am strongly thinking about seeing what fiero's I can find in the j/y that might be reviveable. Anyone have any pointers I should look for? I am assuming I will not find one with a running engine nor am I likely to find one with good interior as most I have seen either have a window open or busted. So I figured I would start by looking for one with a good body. <P>  I figure if I find one with a good body chassis and working suspension I can buy a running motor and work on the interior slowly. <P>  Thought it might be my quickest way into a Fiero  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Randy||||reg||5
Z||000001||Steve Normington||08-07-2001||12:16 AM||||IMHO, NNNNNNOOOOOOOOOOOooooooo!!!!!! Unless you find a rare Fiero (Indy/88 GT T-top/Blue), you're best bet is to get a working Fiero or one that you know exactly what is wrong with it. One from a junkyard will have all sorts of hidden issues. Fieros are not that rare that you shouldn't be able to find one in good shape for your price range.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cheever2||08-07-2001||12:21 AM||||I don't know about it being the quickest way into one, but if that's the way you want (or must) go about it, my advice is to look for one with good wiring and a nice straight frame.  Next would be to look for good front & rear facias, and rear clip (notchback will be best for cheaper parts later, like taillights).  Those large parts don't ship for peanuts, if at all.  And I'd say go for the earlier years, 84 or 85, since more parts will be plentiful.<P>Good luck!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||8Ball||08-07-2001||12:22 AM||||problem is that at this time and for at least the next few months my price range is next to null. I figured it would be alot easier to fund a fiero a dollar at a time rather than a grand up front. If I had the grand I would buy songman's   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><BR>I know where an 85 gt is but didn't look at it all that well so no idea of the shape of everything<p>[This message has been edited by 8Ball (edited 08-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cheever2||08-07-2001||12:41 AM||||AutoTrader.com has Fieros for next-to-null.<BR>The only advantage I can think of for building one piece by piece is you can end up with one very nice spotless machine, and learn a lot in the process (and make some friends).  <BR>Just have a plan ready before you begin.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||maryjane||08-07-2001||02:51 AM||||Can you get a good title for a junkyard car where you live? Not all states allow it.||||reg||5
Z||000006||Songman||08-07-2001||09:32 AM||||Don,<P>Tennessee is very strict on titles. Every car on the road regardless of age must have a title. Where I am from in Georgia, it is not like this. All you need down there is a bill of sale on a car after it is 15 years old and it is yours.<P>I guess it would depend on how a car ended up in a junkyard. If it's wrecked and totalled then you would have to get a salvage title on it, but if I had a car sitting in my yard and sold it to the junkard just to get it gone the title should still be good.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000007||ChadMan||08-07-2001||09:45 AM||||8Ball, save your money!  You can find complete, running 4-cyl Fieros for less than $1000 if you don't mind a cracked panel here and a torn seat there.  (Or you can buy NICE non-running Fieros like Songman's in the same price range.)<P>Hell, if you showed up on my doorstep with $1500 cash, I'd sell you my Coupe.  Paint's starting to go, but it runs good, it's very clean and it's never been wrecked.||||reg||3
Z||000008||Ken Wittlief||08-07-2001||11:11 AM||||As a general rule:<P>the best way to get a good car is to spend as much as you can for a car as a whole, then fix it up from there - ie buy the best car you can afford now and plan on putting money into it as you go along<P>you should almost never buy a car you cant take out for a test drive - there are SO many things that can be wrong with a car that you CANT discover unless you drive it.<P> of course there are exceptions: estate auctions, charity auctions, places where you can hear the car run at least, and get a REALLY great price, like $200 or so -is not a big risk, cause if the car is a lemon you can always use it for parts.<P>  IMHO if you think you will start out cheap by buying a junker you will end up spending more money in the lng run, and it may sit in your driveway for a LONG time before you can get it up to snuff to pass inspection, or to even run at all.||||reg||11
Z||000009||8Ball||08-07-2001||04:29 PM||||Well during an exaustive phone search to locate all the Fiero's in j/y's in the area I found several to look at. But one of them is an 84 2m4 red the clearcoat is faded on the rear the front looks like new. the int. is in baaaaad shape and the drivers side power window won't go down. The windshield is cracked but it started right up. I drove it for a couple miles and it seems to run descent all things considered. Maybe if it is still there when I am thru looking at the rest, I may buy it for 400. It has a clear title also. <P>But like I said I have alot more to look at, about 20 or so.<P>8ball<P>P.S. Of coarse I may say forget it and junk the whole Idea and wait til I can afford a better one. But then I kinda like fixing cars up.||||reg||1
Z||000010||8Ball||08-07-2001||05:56 PM||||Wouldn't ya know it.<P>A guy from a j/y about 40 miles from here just called me he has an 87 gt in very good shape (so he says), says the only thing wrong in the headlight motors are non-functional and it is in need of a tie rod.<BR>Said if I want it tomorrow I can have it for 800 bucks. but after tomorrow he is going to take it to his car lot and sell it.<P>Dern it||||reg||1
Z||000011||rockcrawl||08-07-2001||09:36 PM||||I've  purchased three Fieros from junk yards for $200-$400 each. The first one is still being driven daily, I only had to replace a broken shift fork to get it back on the road. The second one was driven daily until a few years ago and is now being used for a kitcar conversion, It only needed a new ECM to be roadworthy. The third one had an engine fire and was just purchased for parts. I probably doubled my money by selling parts and still have lots of parts left over. So buying from a salvage yard is not all bad, as long as you know what you're looking at and don't spend too much. ||||reg||1
